{
  "meta": {
    "slug": "era5-summer-high-nuts2-2015-2024",
    "label": "Average July daily maximum temperature (2015-2024)",
    "year": 2024,
    "source": "Copernicus Climate Data Store (ERA5-Land)",
    "sourceUrl": "https://cds.climate.copernicus.eu/datasets/derived-era5-land-daily-statistics",
    "extractedAt": "2026-10-03",
    "unit": "celsius",
    "dimensions": [
      "value"
    ],
    "methodNote": "Mean of July's daily maximum 2m temperature, averaged across 2015-2024 and across every ERA5-Land grid cell inside each NUTS 2 region's own boundary. Fixed calendar month (July), same reasoning as the comune-grain layer of the same name.",
    "grain": "nuts2",
    "kind": "scalar",
    "coverageNote": "ERA5-Land's native grid is ~9km (0.1 degree) resolution — each region's figure is the average of every grid cell whose center falls inside that NUTS 2 region's own boundary, not a single nearest cell, since a region can be far larger — or, for a handful of city-regions, smaller — than one grid cell. 2 region(s) — ES64, ES63 — contain no grid cell inside their own polygon at this resolution (small city-regions or island exclaves) and are assigned the nearest LAND cell to their centroid instead — a real ERA5-Land reading, just from a cell slightly outside the region's own boundary, rather than a dropped or fabricated figure. Very small regions (e.g. Brussels-Capital, Malta) may average just one or two grid cells, which is real, not a bug — larger regions can average thousands."
  },
